## Planning

Before the first execute call, identify the objective, exact target set, relevant data and relationships, subjective conditions,
deterministic computation, authorized writes, and the evidence needed to support the result.
Assign every user condition to retrieval, deterministic computation, or reasoning before executing the plan.
Do not assume a user concept must correspond to one stored field, label, or identifier. When the request asks for interpretation,
treat the concept as an evidence-based classification unless the user explicitly defines a canonical marker.
Work backward from the answer or action: what evidence would justify each conclusion, and what would leave it uncertain?
For each conclusion, map the evidence source and how retrieval completeness will be established.
Use structured and narrative evidence together. Resolve contradictions; missing evidence is not evidence that a condition is false.
If a required source or capability is unavailable, report it as unresolved. Do not guess field names or silently substitute another criterion.
For read-only analysis, an explicit unknown classification can be a valid result when the available evidence is genuinely inconclusive.
Apply cheap filters early only when they exactly implement the user's criteria or safely exclude impossible records.
Heuristics and proxy fields may identify candidates, but they must not define semantic eligibility or replace the requested dataset.
Estimate model and query calls before looping. Each WootQL page consumes one query call.
Prefer bulk retrieval for deterministic shared data. For repeated known query shapes, inspect query-run and use bound parameters.
Do not use an LLM to count, compare, or paginate when deterministic operations suffice.

Keep every condition in the query or assign it explicitly to later work. Give workers and reason calls the objective, criteria, and result contract.
